# pastebin (rung 1) — an expired paste, and the sweep that reclaims it.
#
# ExpirePaste is documented as internal-only: dispatched by the app layer's
# expiry sweep, never by a GUI client (pastebin/dto/paste_dto.hpp). It is a
# registered action on a reachable model, though, so a scenario can drive the
# sweep's own step and observe the two distinct states either side of it --
# which no in-process test of the sweep does, because the sweep and the reader
# share a process there.
#
# The three states, in order, each with its own distinct reply:
#   1. present but past its expiry  -> GetPaste refuses, "paste has expired"
#   2. swept                        -> GetPaste refuses, "no such paste"
#   3. swept again                  -> ExpirePaste is a no-op, not an error
#
# ── Why this needs no clock and no sleep ────────────────────────────────────
# The expiry instant is *sent by the scenario*, in the past. Nothing here
# waits for time to pass, so this cannot become the kind of flaky timing test
# morph#147 was. ExpirePaste's own DELETE carries an `expires_at_ms <= now`
# guard, which is what makes it replay-safe and is also why it must be given a
# genuinely expired paste to have any effect at all.
#
# ── A listing fact that is not guessable ────────────────────────────────────
# ListPastes filters on `is_private` and nothing else, so an expired paste is
# still *listed* until the sweep reclaims it, even though it can no longer be
# read. Both halves are asserted below.
#
# Re-runnable: both pastes are created here, with a literal past instant.

model PasteModel

client alice

do CreatePaste content="already stale" syntax=plaintext expiresAt="2020-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"
expect ok capture stale=$.id

# Unreadable on arrival -- and told apart from a paste that never existed.
do GetPaste id=$stale
expect err message == "GetPaste: paste has expired"
expect err message !~ "no such paste"

# Still listed, though: the listing's only filter is privacy.
do ListPastes cursor="$stale~"
expect ok field pastes ~ "\"id\":\"$stale\""

# The sweep's own step.
do ExpirePaste id=$stale
expect ok

# Now genuinely gone, and the refusal changes to say so.
do GetPaste id=$stale
expect err message == "GetPaste: no such paste"
expect err message !~ "expired"

do ListPastes cursor="$stale~"
expect ok field pastes !~ "\"id\":\"$stale\""

# Sweeping what is already swept is a no-op. This is the replay case: the
# journaled entry carries only the id, so re-running it must be harmless.
do ExpirePaste id=$stale
expect ok

# ── The guard bites: the sweep does not touch a paste that has not expired ──
do CreatePaste content="still fresh" syntax=plaintext expiresAt="2999-12-31T23:59:59.000Z"
expect ok capture fresh=$.id

do ExpirePaste id=$fresh
expect ok

# Untouched, which is the whole point of the `expires_at_ms <= now` guard.
do GetPaste id=$fresh
expect ok field content == "still fresh"
